<<<
without any loss of face, & least of all without loss of office.
===
without *any loss of face*, & least of all without *loss of office*.
>>>

<<<
witticism. A HYBRID DERIVATIVE.
===
**witticism.** A ^^HYBRID DERIVATIVE^^.
>>>

<<<
wive. See -yE(D).
===
**wive.** See ^^-VE(D)^^.
>>>

<<<
Wizard. For W. of the North, see SOBRIQUETS. wizened, wizen, weazen. All three forms are or have been used as adjectives, but the first is now usual.
===
**wizard.** For *W.* of the North, see ^^SOBRIQUETS^^.

**wizened, wizen, weazen.** All three forms are or have been used as adjectives, but the first is now usual.
>>>

<<<
The -en of wizen & weazen is a p.p. termination, as well as the -ed of wizened.
===
The -en of *wizen* & *weazen* is a p.p. termination, as well as the -ed of *wizened*.
>>>

<<<
wizier. See VIZIER.
===
**wizier.** See ^^VIZIER^^.
>>>

<<<
wobble, wab-. The first is now the established spelling.
===
**wobble, wab-.** The first is now the established spelling.
>>>

<<<
wolf. See -vE(D).
===
**wolf.** See ^^-VE(D)^^.
>>>

<<<
wolverene, -ine. The first appears to be accepted.
===
**wolverene, -ine.** The first appears to be accepted.
>>>

<<<
woman. For woman suffrage, as a phrase, see FEMALE)(WOMAN. For chairwoman & chairman, as applied to a woman, see FEMININE DESIGNA- TIONS.
===
**woman.** For *woman suffrage*, as a phrase, see ^^FEMALE^^)(^^WOMAN^^. For *chairwoman* & *chairman*, as applied to a woman, see ^^FEMININE DESIGNATIONS^^.
>>>

<<<
Womankind, not womenkind, for the whole sex or women in general; but womenkind is common for one’s female relatives &c.
===
*Womankind*, not *womenkind*, for the whole sex or women in general; but *womenkind* is common for one's female relatives &c.
>>>

<<<
womanly. See FEMALE, FEMININE.
===
**womanly.** See ^^FEMALE^^, ^^FEMININE^^.
>>>

<<<
wonder. For I shouldnt w. if it didn’t rain, see NOT 4, & STURDY INDEFENSIBLES.
===
**wonder.** For *I shouldn't w. if it didn't rain*, see ^^NOT^^ 4, & ^^STURDY INDEFENSIBLES^^.
>>>

<<<
wood. Wood anemone is better as two separate words; also, without question, wood pavement; see Hy- PHENS 3 B.
===
**wood.** *Wood anemone* is better as two separate words; also, without question, *wood pavement*; see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B.
>>>

<<<
Woodbine, not -bind, is the established form, esp. with Shakspere & Milton to maintain it.
===
*Woodbine*, not *-bind*, is the established form, esp. with Shakspere & Milton to maintain it.
>>>

<<<
Tomorrow to fresh woods, not fields; a pasture, by the way, is a field; see MISQUOTATION.
===
*Tomorrow to fresh woods*, not *fields*; a pasture, by the way, *is* a field; see ^^MISQUOTATION^^.
>>>

<<<
wooden makes woodenness.
===
**wooden** makes *woodenness*.
>>>

<<<
woof, warp, web, weft. The warp is a set of parallel threads stretched out; the threads woven across & between these are the woof or weft; & the fabric that results is the web.
===
**woof, warp, web, weft.** The *warp* is a set of parallel threads stretched out; the threads woven across & between these are the *woof* or *weft*; & the fabric that results is the *web*.
>>>

<<<
wool makes, in British spelling, woollen, woolly, & in American woolen, woolly; woollen is perhaps anomalous even by British standards (see -LL-, -L-), but is certainly established;
===
**wool** makes, in British spelling, *woollen, woolly*, & in American *woolen, woolly*; *woollen* is perhaps anomalous even by British standards (see ^^-LL-, -L-^^), but is certainly established;
>>>

<<<
&, on its analogy, -woolled should be better than -wooilcd.
===
&, on its analogy, *-woolled* should be better than *-wooled*.
>>>

<<<
woolly bear.

No hyphen; see HYPHENS 38 B.
===
**woolly bear.** No hyphen; see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B.
>>>

<<<
WORD-PATRONAGE. Under SUPER- IORITY, the tendency to take out one’s words & look at them, to apologize for expressions that either need no apology or should be quietly refrained from, has been mentioned.
===
**WORD-PATRONAGE.** Under ^^SUPERIORITY^^, the tendency to take out one's words & look at them, to apologize for expressions that either need no apology or should be quietly refrained from, has been mentioned.
>>>

<<<
but it is rare enough to deserve this little article all to itself: . . . propose to use their powers to force a dissolution.
===
but it is rare enough to deserve this little article all to itself: . . . *propose to use their powers to force a dissolution.*
>>>

<<<
That is a contingency which has been adumbrated (to revive a word which has been rather neglected of late); but this is one more case in which we must be content to wait & see.
===
*That is a contingency which has* been adumbrated (to revive a word which has been rather neglected of late); *but this is one more case in which we must be content to wait & see.*
>>>

<<<
work, vb. The disappearance of the form wrought is so manifest,
===
**work,** vb. The disappearance of the form *wrought* is so manifest,
>>>

<<<
A few sentences with blanks for wrought or worked will illustrate.
===
A few sentences with blanks for *wrought* or *worked* will illustrate.
>>>

<<<
good time. A contemporary who

in brass. These things have —-— together for good. She —— upon his feelings.
===
good time. *A contemporary who —— in brass. These things have —— together for good. She —— upon his feelings.*
>>>

<<<
This infinite mischief. They have —— their will. Conscience —— within him. He —— his audience into fury. When they were sufficiently —— up.
===
*This —— infinite mischief. They have —— their will. Conscience —— within him. He —— his audience into fury. When they were sufficiently —— up.*
>>>

<<<
workaday is now displaced, wholly in the noun use, & for the most part as an adjective, by the normal workday, of which it is regarded as a slipshod pronunciation to be used only as a genial unbending; ‘this workaday world ° is still usual.
===
**workaday** is now displaced, wholly in the noun use, & for the most part as an adjective, by the normal *workday*, of which it is regarded as a slipshod pronunciation to be used only as a genial unbending; 'this workaday world' is still usual.
>>>

<<<
working. W. capital, w. expenses, &c., should have no hyphens; see Hypuens 3 B.
===
**working.** *W. capital, w. expenses*, &c., should have no hyphens; see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B.
>>>

